{
  "id": "pc-exp",
  "chapterNumber": "6",
  "title": {
    "en": "Exponential & Logarithmic Functions",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Exponential functions model multiplicative change — growth and decay. Logarithms are their inverses and appear naturally in measurement scales (Richter, decibel, pH) and in solving exponential equations.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Properties of exponential functions \\(f(x)=ab^x\\)",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Domain: \\(\\mathbb{R}\\). Range: \\((0,\\infty)\\) when \\(a&gt;0.\\) Horizontal asymptote: \\(y=0.\\)</p>\\[b^m\\cdot b^n=b^{m+n},\\quad\\frac{b^m}{b^n}=b^{m-n},\\quad(b^m)^n=b^{mn},\\quad b^0=1,\\quad b^{-n}=\\frac{1}{b^n}.\\]<p>Natural exponential: \\(f(x)=e^x,\\;e\\approx2.71828.\\)</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Logarithm properties (\\(\\log_b x=y\\iff b^y=x\\))",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[\\log_b(MN)=\\log_b M+\\log_b N,\\quad\\log_b\\!\\left(\\frac{M}{N}\\right)=\\log_b M-\\log_b N,\\]\\[\\log_b(M^p)=p\\log_b M,\\quad\\log_b x=\\frac{\\log x}{\\log b}=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln b}.\\]<p>\\(\\log_b 1=0,\\;\\log_b b=1,\\;b^{\\log_b x}=x,\\;\\log_b(b^x)=x.\\)</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Growth and decay models",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[P(t)=P_0\\,b^t,\\quad P(t)=P_0\\,e^{rt}\\quad(r&gt;0:\\text{growth};\\;r&lt;0:\\text{decay}).\\]\\[\\text{Half-life: }A(t)=A_0\\!\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/h}.\\quad\\text{Compound: }A=P\\!\\left(1+\\frac{r}{n}\\right)^{nt}.\\]<p><strong>Logistic growth:</strong> \\(P(t)=\\dfrac{K}{1+Ae^{-bt}}\\) where \\(K\\) is the carrying capacity.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
